add(H1('{{c:abstract}}Abstract{{/c}}'));
add(P('Copper (Cu) accumulates in agricultural soils, yet it remains unclear how arbuscular mycorrhizal (AM) fungi respond at the low end of the contamination range, and whether in vitro assays predict responses in the presence of a whole plant. We exposed *Rhizophagus intraradices* GC3 to Cu in a soil-based, bi-compartmented in vitro system with Ri T-DNA carrot roots (n = 3) and in a greenhouse experiment with sunflower (*Helianthus annuus*; n = 4), each inoculated or not. In vitro, Cu in the hyphal compartment (measured 100, about 180 and about 290 mg kg^−1^) did not alter root colonisation but changed fungal development: soil sporulation increased at the intermediate dose (2.8-fold; 95% CI 1.1–7.2) and collapsed at the highest (0.06-fold; 0.02–0.15), to values indistinguishable from the non-inoculated background, whereas spores in the Cu-free root compartment did not decline. In vivo, Cu did not affect plant biomass, colonisation or soil hyphal length. Inoculation raised easily extractable glomalin-related soil protein at all doses (1.5- to 2.0-fold), with no dose–response, and total protein only modestly; neither fraction responded to inoculation in vitro, which suggests that the in vivo increase is not a direct measure of fungal production. Sunflower behaved as a Cu excluder (bioconcentration factor < 1), and plants held < 0.5% of the soil Cu pool. Colonisation was a poor indicator of Cu stress, whereas sporulation in soil was highly sensitive. Our results support a metal-avoidance response of GC3 rather than a Cu-induced increase in glomalin.'));
add(PL('**Keywords:** arbuscular mycorrhizal fungi; copper; glomalin-related soil protein; *Rhizophagus intraradices*; root organ culture; sporulation; sunflower; trace elements'));

add(H1('1. Introduction'));
add(P('Copper (Cu) is an essential micronutrient that can become a {{c:term}}potentially toxic trace element{{/c}} in soil (Duffus 2002; Pourret and Hursthouse 2019). Fungicides, fertilisers and other agricultural inputs have raised its concentration in cultivated land: in a survey of European topsoils, mean Cu reached 49 mg kg^−1^ in vineyards against an overall mean of 17 mg kg^−1^ (Ballabio et al. 2018; see also Tóth et al. 2016). Excess Cu impairs root growth and nutrient acquisition, particularly of phosphorus (P), and its effects can be modulated by soil P supply (Brunetto et al. 2016; Baldi et al. 2018; Feil et al. 2020). {{c:efsa}}Its transfer to crops is also of dietary concern{{/c}}: the European Food Safety Authority recently set an acceptable daily intake of 0.07 mg kg^−1^ body weight, with food and pesticide use among the contributing sources (EFSA Scientific Committee 2023).'));
add(P('Arbuscular mycorrhizal (AM) fungi contribute to the uptake of low-mobility nutrients, including P and Cu, and modulate plant metal acquisition across a broad range of soil concentrations (Parniske 2008; Ferrol et al. 2016). They may alleviate metal toxicity through binding to hyphal walls, compartmentalisation in vesicles and spores, and active efflux (Hildebrandt et al. 2007; González-Guerrero et al. 2008; Cornejo et al. 2013; Janeeshma and Puthur 2020; Gómez-Gallego et al. 2024). Avoidance of contaminated patches has also been proposed (Pawlowska and Charvat 2004). The outcome for the host depends on the combination of fungus, plant and Cu level (Meier et al. 2012; Merlos et al. 2016; Colombo et al. 2024), and much of the evidence comes from heavily polluted sites (Cornejo et al. 2008; Colombo et al. 2020; Benavidez et al. 2024). Less is known about slightly contaminated soils.'));
add(P('Glomalin-related soil protein (GRSP) is frequently invoked in this context. It has been related to soil aggregation and to the binding of metals (Wright and Upadhyaya 1998; Rillig 2004; González-Chávez et al. 2004; Cornejo et al. 2008; Son et al. 2024), and glomalin yield has been reported to increase under some suboptimal conditions (Rillig and Steinberg 2002; Hammer and Rillig 2011). However, GRSP is defined operationally: hot citrate extracts contain heat-stable proteins and other compounds of non-mycorrhizal origin, and Bradford-based quantification overestimates glomalin (Rosier et al. 2006; Gillespie et al. 2011; Holátko et al. 2021). Axenic controls are therefore valuable for separating fungal production from background.'));
add(P('Root organ cultures offer such control, but traditionally use synthetic media. The soil-based system developed by Silvani et al. (2019) exposes the extraradical mycelium (ERM) to a real soil matrix while keeping the root in a separate compartment. With this system, Cu has been mapped in intraradical structures of *R. intraradices* GC3 by microPIXE (Statello et al. 2024), but the developmental response of the fungus to a Cu gradient, and whether it anticipates the response in the presence of a whole plant in soil, have not been evaluated. Sunflower is a suitable host for this comparison: it is a crop species, and its response to Cu and AM fungi has been examined previously (Castañón-Silva et al. 2013; Meier et al. 2012).'));
add(P('Here, we compared the response of *R. intraradices* GC3 to Cu in the soil-based in vitro system and in sunflower grown in the same type of soil. {{c:hyp}}We hypothesised that{{/c}} (i) Cu would reduce extraradical growth and sporulation in soil before affecting root colonisation; (ii) GRSP would increase with Cu; (iii) inoculation would limit the transfer of Cu from soil to shoots; and (iv) the direction of the responses shared by both systems would agree.'));

add(H1('2. Materials and methods'));
add(H2('2.1. Biological material and soil'));
add(P('*Rhizophagus intraradices* (N.C. Schenck & G.S. Sm.) C. Walker & A. Schüßler, strain GC3, was provided by the Banco de Glomeromycota In Vitro (BGIV, www.bgiv.com.ar) and maintained with Ri T-DNA transformed carrot (*Daucus carota* L.) roots on Minimal Medium (MM; Bécard and Fortin 1988). Sunflower (*Helianthus annuus* L. var. Mammoth Russian; {{c:seed}}==[seed size]=={{/c}}) was the host in vivo. Both assays used {{c:soil}}a Mollisol from Buenos Aires province{{/c}} (silt loam: 12% sand, 65.5% silt, 22.5% clay; pH 5.1; total C 14.9 g kg^−1^; total N 1.53 g kg^−1^; P 13.9 mg kg^−1^ ==[extraction method]==).'));
add(H2('2.2. In vitro assay'));
add(P('The assay followed Silvani et al. (2019), using {{c:plate}}bi-compartmented Petri plates (90 × 14 mm){{/c}} that separated a root compartment (RC) from a hyphal compartment (HC; St-Arnaud et al. 1996). The RC received 30 mL of MM and a 5-cm non-mycorrhizal root fragment, inoculated with a plug of a 4-month-old monoxenic culture containing approximately 200 spores, mycelium and colonised roots. The HC received 30 mL of sterile dry soil, homogeneously amended with {{c:doseVitro}}4 mL of filter-sterilised (0.2 µm) CuSO~4~ solution (Cu1: 150 mg L^−1^; Cu2: 300 mg L^−1^) or sterile deionised water (Cu0){{/c}}. Cu1 corresponded to values recorded in soils of the Riachuelo basin, Buenos Aires (Mendoza et al. 2015; Colombo et al. 2020). Non-inoculated (NI) plates received only the root fragment. The six treatments (GC3 or NI × Cu0, Cu1 or Cu2) had three replicates each and were incubated in the dark at 25 °C for {{c:times}}120 days{{/c}}.'));
add(H2('2.3. In vivo assay'));
add(P('Seeds were surface-sterilised (70% v/v ethanol, 20 min; 20% v/v sodium hypochlorite with 0.1% Tween 20, 30 min), rinsed with sterile distilled water and germinated on moist filter paper at 25 °C in the dark for 4 days. Three seedlings were sown in 1.5-L pots with tyndallised soil and thinned to one three days after emergence. Inoculated plants received a 1-cm^3^ plug of 4-month-old monoxenic culture; NI plants received a plug of MM with non-mycorrhizal transformed roots only. Pots were watered by capillarity with deionised water and fertilised every 15 days with 100 mL of P-free Hewitt solution (Hewitt 1952). After 30 days, each pot received {{c:doseVivo}}420 mL of CuSO~4~ solution (Cu1: 150 mg L^−1^; Cu2: 300 mg L^−1^) or sterile deionised water (Cu0){{/c}}. The 24 pots (six treatments, n = 4) were randomised in a greenhouse under natural light and temperature ==[dates, mean temperature]== and harvested 130 days after sowing.'));
add(H2('2.4. Harvest and fungal variables'));
add(P('Shoots and roots were separated and dried at 70 °C for 48 h to determine dry weight. Root samples from both assays were cleared (10% w/v KOH, 90 °C, 15 min), acidified (0.1 N HCl, 5 min) and stained with 0.02% w/v trypan blue in lactic acid (90 °C, 10 min; modified from Phillips and Hayman 1970). Frequency (%F) and intensity (%I) of colonisation were estimated according to Declerck et al. (2004) under a light microscope at 400× on {{c:frag}}root fragments mounted in groups of 10{{/c}}; in the in vitro assay, arbuscules (%A), vesicles (%V) and intraradical spores (%IS) were also recorded. One gram of dry soil from each pot or HC was shaken with 100 mL of 0.002% trypan blue for 10 min, and aliquots were poured on a 9-cm Petri dish with a 1-cm^2^ grid; hyphal length was calculated from grid intersections (Newman 1966). In the RC, hyphal length and spores were counted in five randomly chosen 1-cm^2^ squares under a stereomicroscope (4.5×). Newly formed spores in soil were counted per gram of dry soil. Because RC and HC values are expressed in different units, the compartments were compared only through relative changes (section 2.6).'));
add(H2('2.5. GRSP and elemental analyses'));
add(P('{{c:bradford}}Bradford-reactive GRSP was determined following Cornejo et al. (2008){{/c}}. Easily extractable GRSP (EE-GRSP) was extracted from 1 g of dry soil with 8 mL of 20 mM citrate (pH 7.0; 121 °C, 30 min). Total GRSP (T-GRSP) was obtained by repeated extraction of 1 g of dry soil with 8 mL of 50 mM citrate (pH 8.0; 121 °C, 60 min), up to five cycles, until the reddish-brown colour disappeared. Extracts were centrifuged (3000 rpm, 15 min), and T-GRSP extracts were pooled and centrifuged again (10,000 rpm, 10 min). Protein was quantified by the Bradford method (Bradford 1976) with bovine serum albumin as standard. {{c:grspVol}}==[State the volume and dilution factors used to express results per gram of soil.]=={{/c}}'));
add(P('Cu and P concentrations in soil and plant material were determined by energy-dispersive X-ray fluorescence on pressed pellets (Custo et al. 2005). Samples were dried at 70 °C for 48 h, milled and sieved (850 µm). {{c:xrf}}==[Report instrument, limits of detection and quantification, certified reference materials and recoveries.]=={{/c}} {{c:pair}}For each pot{{/c}}, we calculated the bioconcentration factor (BCF = element concentration in shoot or root / concentration in soil), the translocation factor (TF = concentration in shoot / concentration in root) and the plant Cu content (concentration × dry weight, summed over shoot and root). The soil Cu pool per pot was estimated from soil concentration and the soil mass recorded at harvest.'));
add(H2('2.6. Statistical analysis'));
add(P('{{c:stats}}Analyses were performed in R 4.6.1{{/c}} (R Core Team 2026). Continuous responses measured in inoculated and NI units were analysed with generalised linear models (Gamma, log link) with inoculation, Cu dose and their interaction, using type II F tests (package car; Fox and Weisberg 2019). Percentages were analysed in inoculated units with quasi-binomial models. Hyphal length and spore numbers per compartment were analysed with linear models on log-transformed values, which are conservative with n = 3. To test whether Cu shifted development between compartments independently of their units, we analysed a per-plate allocation index, log(HC/RC), which is equivalent to the compartment × Cu interaction on the log scale. Results are reported as ratios of means with 95% confidence intervals (CI; emmeans; Lenth 2025). To compare systems, we estimated unit-free ratios (Cu2/Cu0 and GC3/NI) and tested the corresponding system × treatment interaction. Multiplicity was controlled with the Benjamini–Hochberg procedure (BH; Benjamini and Hochberg 1995), both across all model terms within each system and within a primary family defined a priori (T-GRSP, EE-GRSP and soil, root and shoot Cu in vivo). Model fit was assessed with simulated residuals (DHARMa; Hartig 2025). Key contrasts were checked with exact permutation tests, with the minimum attainable P noted where relevant. The multivariate response in vivo was assessed by PERMANOVA on standardised log-transformed variables (vegan; Oksanen et al. 2025). {{c:exclude}}Data handling decisions are documented in the accompanying code.{{/c}} ==[Data exclusions to be confirmed.]=='));

add(H1('3. Results'));
add(H2('3.1. Copper measured in the substrate'));
add(P('In vitro, soil Cu increased with dose from about 100 mg kg^−1^ in Cu0 to 158 ± 34 (GC3) and 198 ± 7 (NI) mg kg^−1^ in Cu1 and to 299 ± 27 and 278 ± 5 mg kg^−1^ in Cu2 (P < 0.001), with no effect of inoculation (Table 1, Fig. 4a). In vivo, soil Cu was 49 ± 3 mg kg^−1^ in NI-Cu0 and rose to 80 ± 18 and 149 ± 32 mg kg^−1^ in NI-Cu2 and GC3-Cu2, respectively (Cu: P = 0.017; Table 3, Fig. 4b). {{c:whyCu1}}Elemental analyses in vivo were performed only on Cu0 and Cu2{{/c}} ==[state the reason]==.'));
add(H2('3.2. In vitro assay'));
add(P('*R. intraradices* GC3 developed in both compartments at all doses (Fig. 1). Root length was unaffected by Cu (P = 0.54; Table 1). Cu did not change %F (84–91%), %I (41–50%) or %A (60–74%) (P ≥ 0.13). Vesicles and intraradical spores tended to be less abundant with Cu (%V: 59, 28 and 36%; P = 0.083; %IS: 81, 34 and 57%; P = 0.057; Table 1).'));
add(P('In contrast, development in the HC responded strongly and non-monotonically to Cu (Fig. 2). Soil spore numbers differed among doses (F~2,6~ = 54.4, P < 0.001): relative to Cu0, they increased 2.8-fold in Cu1 (95% CI 1.1–7.2; P = 0.034) and fell to 0.06-fold in Cu2 (0.02–0.15; P < 0.001). The value in GC3-Cu2 (9 ± 2 g^−1^) did not exceed the {{c:bgHyph}}background of NI plates (7 ± 1 g^−1^){{/c}}. Hyphal length in the HC followed the same pattern with less amplitude (F~2,6~ = 7.4, P = 0.024): 1.8-fold in Cu1 (1.1–3.1; P = 0.031) and 0.83-fold in Cu2 (0.49–1.39; P = 0.41).'));
add(P('In the Cu-free RC, spore numbers varied in the opposite direction (F~2,5~ = 11.5, P = 0.013): 0.44-fold in Cu1 (0.19–1.04; P = 0.057) and 1.8-fold in Cu2 (0.78–4.3; P = 0.13). Consequently, the spore allocation index log(HC/RC) changed markedly with dose (F~2,5~ = 39.3, P < 0.001), rising 6.8-fold in Cu1 (1.2–38) and falling to 0.035-fold in Cu2 (0.006–0.20). The hyphal allocation index showed a similar, non-significant trend (F~2,6~ = 4.7, P = 0.060); hyphal length in the RC was 3241 ± 453 cm in Cu2 compared with 1307 ± 413 cm in Cu0. Excluding two questionable plates did not modify the spore pattern.'));
add(P('T-GRSP (20–24 µg g^−1^) and EE-GRSP (3–10 µg g^−1^) in the HC were unaffected by inoculation (P = 0.27 and 0.99) or Cu (P = 0.44 and 0.47; Fig. 3a, c). A T-GRSP inoculation × Cu interaction (P = 0.033) reflected higher values in NI-Cu1 and did not survive BH correction (P = 0.20). Total soil P was slightly lower in GC3-Cu2 than in NI-Cu2 (363 ± 27 vs 413 ± 3 mg kg^−1^; ratio 0.88, 0.78–0.99; P = 0.040, BH-adjusted P = 0.17).'));
add(H2('3.3. In vivo assay'));
add(P('Cu did not affect sunflower biomass: total dry weight ranged from 9.5 to 12.1 g, with no effects of inoculation, Cu or their interaction (P ≥ 0.21; Table 2). The shoot:root ratio increased with Cu (P = 0.040). GC3 colonised sunflower roots at all doses (%F 77–80%; %I 18–22%; P ≥ 0.72). Soil hyphal length tended to decrease with Cu (80 ± 11, 66 ± 17 and 51 ± 8 cm g^−1^; Cu2/Cu0 = 0.63, 0.34–1.19; P = 0.13). No AM fungal hyphae were found in NI pots, and no extraradical spores were detected in any treatment. {{c:aggr}}==[Soil aggregation: not reported; see comment.]=={{/c}}'));
add(P('Inoculation increased EE-GRSP (F~1,18~ = 46.2, P < 0.001; BH-adjusted P < 0.001): the GC3/NI ratio was 1.45 (1.08–1.96), 1.90 (1.41–2.56) and 1.98 (1.47–2.67) in Cu0, Cu1 and Cu2. Inoculation also increased T-GRSP (F~1,18~ = 8.7, P = 0.009; BH-adjusted P = 0.065), with ratios of 1.30 (0.98–1.71), 1.03 (0.78–1.36) and 1.46 (1.11–1.92; P = 0.010). {{c:tgrsp}}Neither fraction showed a Cu effect or an inoculation × Cu interaction{{/c}} (P ≥ 0.19; Fig. 3b, d); within inoculated pots, the Cu2/Cu0 ratio was 1.20 (0.91–1.59; P = 0.17) for T-GRSP and 1.10 (0.82–1.49; P = 0.49) for EE-GRSP.'));
add(P('Shoot Cu concentrations (22–32 mg kg^−1^) did not differ among treatments (Table 3, Fig. 4d). {{c:rootCu}}Root Cu was 1.6-fold higher in GC3-Cu2 than in NI-Cu2{{/c}} (19.3 ± 1.3 vs 12.2 ± 1.9 mg kg^−1^; 1.05–2.36; P = 0.031; permutation P = 0.11), while the interaction was marginal (P = 0.068; Fig. 4c). {{c:soilCu}}Soil Cu tended to be higher in GC3-Cu2 than in NI-Cu2{{/c}} (ratio 1.87, 0.90–3.88; P = 0.087). {{c:bcf}}BCF values were below 1 in all treatments{{/c}} (root 0.14–0.31; shoot 0.23–0.56), and {{c:tf}}TF exceeded 1{{/c}} (1.3–2.6), with a tendency towards lower values in inoculated plants (P = 0.069). Plant Cu content was 194–380 µg per plant, less than 0.5% of the estimated soil Cu pool (71–187 mg per pot), and doubled with Cu only in inoculated plants (Cu2/Cu0: GC3 1.96, 1.01–3.80, P = 0.047; NI 1.01, 0.55–1.88, P = 0.96). {{c:pendP}}==[Tissue P results withheld pending verification of the XRF values; see comment and Table S1.]=={{/c}} Soil P did not differ among treatments (635–671 mg kg^−1^).'));
add(P('Considered jointly, biomass, GRSP and Cu variables responded to inoculation (PERMANOVA: R^2^ = 0.20, P = 0.011) and to Cu (R^2^ = 0.15, P = 0.039), without interaction (P = 0.16; n = 13).'));
add(H2('3.4. Comparison between systems'));
add(P('Unit-free ratios indicated that the effect of Cu on colonisation and soil hyphal length had the same direction in both systems, and the system × Cu interaction was not significant for any fungal variable or GRSP fraction (P ≥ 0.20; Fig. 5a). The systems differed in the response of GRSP to inoculation (Fig. 5b): the inoculation × system interaction was significant for EE-GRSP (P < 0.001) and T-GRSP (P = 0.038). Spore production could not be compared because no extraradical spores formed in vivo.'));

add(H1('4. Discussion'));
add(H2('4.1. Sporulation in soil, not colonisation, reflected the Cu gradient'));
add(P('Our first hypothesis was partly supported. Root colonisation was insensitive to Cu in both systems, in agreement with the tolerance described for *R. intraradices* and related fungi (Ferrol et al. 2016), and would have led to the conclusion that Cu had no effect. Sporulation in soil, by contrast, differed about 50-fold between Cu1 and Cu2. {{c:avoid}}This pattern is consistent with the findings of Pawlowska and Charvat (2004){{/c}}, who reported that, in compartmented cultures of *G. intraradices*, inhibition by Cd, Pb and Zn was limited to the metal-enriched compartment, that sporulation was more sensitive than mycelial expansion, and that these features could reflect a metal-avoidance strategy. Here, the response extends to Cu in a soil matrix: at the highest dose, soil sporulation dropped to background while spores in the Cu-free compartment did not decrease.'));
add(P('The stimulation observed at the intermediate dose resembles hormesis (Calabrese and Baldwin 2003), although Cu is also a micronutrient, and a nutritional effect cannot be excluded. The in vitro design cannot separate a true redirection of resources from independent responses of each compartment, and replication was low; the allocation shift should therefore be confirmed with greater replication and more doses, ideally with intermediate steps between Cu1 and Cu2 to locate the threshold. Testing whether spores formed under Cu accumulate the metal, as reported for other fungi (Cornejo et al. 2013; Benavidez et al. 2024), would help to distinguish avoidance from compartmentalisation, which need not be mutually exclusive. Efflux, recently shown to be a major detoxification route in the ERM of *R. irregularis* (Gómez-Gallego et al. 2024), could also contribute. From an ecotoxicological perspective, sporulation in soil may be a more informative endpoint than colonisation.'));
add(H2('4.2. No evidence that copper increased GRSP'));
add(P('Our data did not support the second hypothesis. Neither GRSP fraction increased with Cu within inoculated units, in either system. The increase with inoculation in vivo was consistent across doses for EE-GRSP and, although largest at Cu2 for T-GRSP, was not accompanied by a significant interaction. This agrees with the lack of a direct effect of Pb contamination on GRSP in a recent field gradient (Cáceres-Mago et al. 2025) and contrasts with the stress-induced increases reported for specific conditions in vitro (Rillig and Steinberg 2002; Hammer and Rillig 2011), which were themselves stressor-dependent.'));
add(P('The lack of an inoculation effect in the axenic HC, despite abundant hyphae, deserves attention. First, about 80% of glomalin produced in vitro remains in hyphae and spores and is released mainly upon degradation (Driver et al. 2005), a process that would be limited under axenic conditions. Second, the citrate–Bradford procedure co-extracts proteins and other compounds of non-mycorrhizal origin (Rosier et al. 2006; Gillespie et al. 2011; Holátko et al. 2021), which is consistent with the substantial signal in NI units; in vivo, inoculation could also increase this signal indirectly by altering root inputs or microbial activity in a non-sterile rhizosphere. Third, the systems differ in host species and carbon supply, soil volume and duration. Thus, the in vivo increase should not be read as a direct measure of fungal glomalin production, and evaluating immunoreactive protein, or an independent marker of AM fungal biomass, is needed. {{c:micro}}Previous microPIXE mapping{{/c}} in this system showed Cu mainly in vesicles and, to a lesser extent, in colonised roots (Statello et al. 2024); the present data add no direct evidence linking GRSP to Cu binding, which would require quantifying Cu in purified extracts (Cornejo et al. 2008; Cáceres-Mago et al. 2025).'));
add(H2('4.3. Copper partitioning: an excluder host with limited extraction'));
add(P('The third hypothesis was not supported: inoculation did not lower shoot Cu. Sunflower tolerated the added Cu without growth reduction, with shoot concentrations far below the 300 mg kg^−1^ threshold proposed for Cu hyperaccumulation (van der Ent et al. 2013) and BCF < 1 throughout; they were close to the {{c:adriano}}5–30 mg kg^−1^ regarded as adequate for crop tissues (Adriano 2001){{/c}}. The amount extracted by plants was negligible relative to the soil pool, so these data do not support phytoextraction. The tendency towards higher root Cu and lower TF in inoculated plants is compatible with the retention of Cu in mycorrhizal roots (Ferrol et al. 2016; Statello et al. 2024), but it was not robust, and effects of AM fungi on Cu partitioning are known to vary with the host and the fungal inoculum (Meier et al. 2012; Merlos et al. 2016). TF values above 1 are not usual for Cu, although low uptake with translocation to the shoot has been described for GC3 in another host (Colombo et al. 2024); this result should be confirmed with analytical methods validated for tissue at these concentrations. Given the lack of growth effects, the Cu level reached in pots should be regarded as slight contamination rather than a stress condition for this host.'));
add(P('Total soil Cu cannot demonstrate immobilisation. Soil Cu did not vary with inoculation in the closed plates, and the higher mean in inoculated pots, which was not statistically supported, cannot be attributed to plant uptake given its magnitude; spatial heterogeneity in pots watered by capillarity is a plausible explanation. Estimating bioavailable and bound fractions, through DTPA extraction (Lindsay and Norvell 1978) and sequential extraction (Rauret et al. 1999) in rhizosphere and bulk soil, would provide a direct test.'));
add(H2('4.4. What the in vitro system predicted, and what it did not'));
add(P('The fourth hypothesis was supported only in part. The in vitro system correctly anticipated the insensitivity of colonisation and the direction of the Cu effect on soil hyphae at the highest dose, but it did not predict the inoculation effect on GRSP, and spore production could not be compared because sunflower pots yielded no extraradical spores. Direct comparison of absolute values is inappropriate because system is fully confounded with host species, Cu exposure route, duration and extraction conditions. We therefore suggest using ratios within each system. Soil-based in vitro assays may be most useful for detecting fungal developmental responses that are less visible in pots, whereas plant-level outcomes require whole-plant experiments.'));
add(P('This study has limitations. Replication was low and several tissue variables had only three values per treatment, so non-significant results do not demonstrate absence of effects. Only total concentrations were measured, a single strain and host were tested, and vegetative tissues alone were analysed, so no inference can be drawn about Cu in edible seed, which is influenced by P supply and AM colonisation in other crops (Zhang et al. 2020).'));
add(H1('5. Conclusions'));
add(P('In a soil with low-level Cu contamination, *R. intraradices* GC3 maintained root colonisation, while its sporulation in soil responded strongly to the Cu gradient, with an increase at the intermediate dose and a collapse at the highest. These results are compatible with metal avoidance and identify sporulation as a sensitive endpoint. Inoculation increased Bradford-reactive GRSP in vivo independently of Cu and had no effect in the axenic system, so these data do not support Cu-induced glomalin production. Sunflower behaved as a Cu excluder, with negligible extraction. Confirming immobilisation will require analyses of Cu fractions and of Cu bound to purified GRSP.'));

